 at a frantic few days in  world football as fifa was convinced to  take a stand  russia hosted the last men's world cup  in 2018  russian president vladimir putin and  fifa boss johnny infantino acted like  best friends  even then many in football were  uncomfortable with russia hosting a  world cup following its 2014 annexation  of crimea  now after the invasion of ukraine  throwing russia out of this year's  tournament in qatar in november and  december has become the big topic for  the globe's biggest sport even if russia  had not even qualified yet  fifa condemns as well  the use of force  by russia and ukraine  violence is never  a solution  fifa initially said russia could no  longer hold any international matches at  home  with neutral venues used without any  fans no russian flags or anthems would  be permitted and the national team would  be known as football union of russia  rather than russia  but this was not enough of a punishment  for the invasion of ukraine according to  poland sweden  and the czech republic  those nations were future or potential  opponents for the russians in march's  playoffs for qatar  after consultations with our players on  the polish national team  the decision was made that it does not  matter in what location we do not want  to play against russia and this way we  want to show our protest against putin's  aggression against ukraine  england followed suit in saying they  would not play russia for the  foreseeable future this all gave fifa no  real wiggle room when it came to russia  playing at the world cup with the  international olympic committee also  adding to the pressure  despite infantino's close bond with  putin he's been left with little choice  [Music]  let's get a global interview now from  the group global athlete cara o'donovan  is a former world champion kickboxer and  now speaking to uh speaking two and four  world-class athletes around the world  thank you very much for joining us and  welcome to the show  we're seeing some unprecedented  decisions considering how reluctant  global sports has been in the past to  punish russia is it too little too late  in your eyes  yeah i think like while there's been a  huge amount of statements coming out  from different sports federations i  think first of all we have to look at  what the detail says and like the  olympics for example on monday they  really did pass the book they haven't  actually sanctioned the olympic  committees of russia and belarus and so  we need it needs to go further and it's  definitely late in the day um so we we  have to see more sanctions um starting  with the banning of athletes in russia  our farm russia and belarus in the  paralympic games will start this week  some say that although this is part of  sports washing i mean could you tell us  what that is exactly and how it works  right well sports washing is certainly  not a new concept it is used by a lot of  authoritarian regimes to cover up for  example human rights abuses and i know  beijing olympics and paralympics have  been widely criticized for the use of  sports watching um sport has been an  enormous part of russia's political  agenda both you know domestically and  internationally and  so that's why we are calling on um  severe sanctions on russia and belarus  because they will use these games they  will use sport  to cover up um  to cover up unjust actions at home and  to use this as part of their agenda and  it's something that we are seeing more  and more of because the likes of sports  the ioc in particular they've been  complicit in this they've been very very  happy to sit by and allow these types of  countries um use sport um for all the  wrong reasons  you say that they've been complicit um  do you think international sporting  bodies have actually learned from this  do you think they will act differently  when they host countries in the future  it's you know it's hard to answer that  question there's part of me that's a  little opti